    98	<exit-code> 0 </exit-code>
    99	```
   100	
   101	The mandated LLVM build warns about several unrelated non-exhaustive functions
   102	in the supplied semantics. A nonempty LLVM probe reaches the intentionally
   103	evaluator-free primitive and exits `113`:
   104	
   105	```text
   106	[Error] krun: runtime-kompiled/interpreter ...
   107	md5hexCodes ( iCons ( 72 , ... iCons ( 100 , .IntSeq ) ... ) )
   108	EXPECTED_OPAQUE_LLVM_FAILURE exit=113
   109	```
   110	
   111	The Haskell execution can preserve that opaque value and completes:
   112	
   113	```bash
   114	krun smoke.mpy --definition verification-kompiled
   115	```
   116	
   117	Actual exit: `0`; its final scope contains both:
   118	
   119	```text
   120	"empty_result" |-> noneV
   121	"digest_result" |-> str ( md5hexCodes ( iCons ( 72 , ... ) ) )
   122	```
   123	
   124	Symbolic build and all required positive target proofs:
   125	
   126	```bash
   127	kompile --backend haskell verification.k \
   128	  --main-module VERIFICATION \
   129	  --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \
   130	  --output-definition verification-kompiled
   131	
   132	kprove spec.k \
   133	  --definition verification-kompiled \
   134	  --spec-module SPEC \
   135	  --claims SPEC.empty-input
   136	
   137	kprove spec.k \
   138	  --definition verification-kompiled \
   139	  --spec-module SPEC \
   140	  --claims SPEC.nonempty-input
   141	
   142	kprove spec.k \
   143	  --definition verification-kompiled \
   144	  --spec-module SPEC
   145	```
   146	
   147	Actual exits: `0`, `0`, `0`, `0`. Each of the three `kprove` commands printed:
   148	
   149	```text
   150	#Top
   151	```
   152	
   153	False-postcondition probe:
   154	
   155	```bash
   156	kprove spec-vacuity.k \
   157	  --definition verification-kompiled \
   158	  --spec-module SPEC-VACUITY
   159	```
   160	
   161	Actual exit: `1`, with `WarnStuckClaimState`. The residual final scope has
   162	`"result" |-> noneV`, which cannot match the deliberately false
   163	`"result" |-> 0`. `prove.sh` records:
   164	
   165	```text
   166	EXPECTED_VACUITY_FAILURE exit=1
   167	```
   168	
   169	Body-sensitivity probe:
   170	
   171	```bash
   172	kprove spec-body-mutation.k \
   173	  --definition verification-kompiled \
   174	  --spec-module SPEC-BODY-MUTATION
   175	```
   176	
   177	Actual exit: `1`, with `WarnStuckClaimState`. The material mutation replaces
   178	the nonempty branch's digest return with `Return(NoneVal)` while retaining the
   179	original digest postcondition for the realizable input `"a"`. The residual has
   180	`"result" |-> noneV`. `prove.sh` records:
   181	
   182	```text
   183	EXPECTED_BODY_MUTATION_FAILURE exit=1
   184	```
   185	
   186	Independent differential evidence:
   187	
   188	```bash
   189	python3 test_solution.py
   190	```
   191	
   192	Actual exit: `0`; output:
   193	
   194	```text
   195	DIFFERENTIAL_CASES=213 MISMATCHES=0
   196	PROMPT_EXAMPLE=3e25960a79dbc69b674cd4ec67a72c62
   197	UTF8_BOUNDARY text='π' codepoints=[960] bytes=[207, 128]
   198	```
   199	
   200	## Gate results
   201	
   202	Gate A — PASS.
   203	
   204	- A1: The exact program body executes under fixed semantics. The claims start
   205	  from module load and include the exact closure body, binding, argument, and
   206	  environment. The body-mutation probe is rejected.
   207	- A2: No proof-local execution is skipped. Every configuration cell is
   208	  explicitly constrained before and after execution.
   209	- A3: Fixed rules perform the function lookup, argument evaluation, parameter
   210	  binding, branch, return, and control-stack restoration. The supplied
   211	  standard-library route is isolated as a named external trust boundary.
   212	- A4: There are no proof-local equations, total functions, or simplification
   213	  rules to check for truth, overlap, coverage, or descent.
   214	- A5: Empty input and concrete nonempty input `"a"` are realizable witnesses.
   215	  The false-postcondition mutation is rejected with a stuck residual showing
   216	  the actual result.
   217	
   218	Gate B — PASS.
   219	
   220	- B1: Empty plus arbitrary nonempty `IntSeq` claims cover the complete modeled
   221	  string domain without a size bound.
   222	- B2: The solution uses real CPython UTF-8 and is faithful on Unicode. The
   223	  supplied K model is explicitly ASCII-oriented and treats encoding as an
   224	  identity on code sequences. This is a fixed-model boundary, not a theorem
   225	  restriction; the theorem covers every `IntSeq` admitted by that model. The
   226	  concrete `π` code-point/UTF-8-byte divergence is recorded.
   227	- B3: The formal result is the fixed semantics' exact opaque
   228	  `md5hexCodes(CS)` summary. Its interpretation as the lowercase MD5 digest is
   229	  conditional on the named supplied primitive contract and independently
   230	  tested; it is not installed as a proof equation.
   231	- B4: `solution.py` implements the prompt directly, returns `None` exactly for
   232	  empty strings, and uses CPython's UTF-8 bytes and lowercase `hexdigest` for
   233	  every nonempty string.
   234	
   235	Gate C — PASS.
   236	
   237	- Every unproved component and dependent claim is listed below.
   238	- Every reported command is present in `prove.sh`; complete output is present
   239	  in `proof-run.log`.
   240	- Formal results, conditional trust, finite evidence, and excluded behavior
   241	  are separated.
   242	
   243	## Trust boundary
   244	
   245	### Supplied MD5 route and value
   246	
   247	- Extension: fixed rules
   248	  `Call(Attribute(Name("hashlib"), "md5"), (E, .Exprs)) => E ~> #md5`,
   249	  `str(CS) ~> #md5 => md5Obj(CS)`,
   250	  `applyMethod(md5Obj(CS), "hexdigest", .Vals) =>
   251	  str(md5hexCodes(CS))`, and the evaluator-free declaration
   252	  `md5hexCodes(IntSeq)`.
   253	- Class: trusted primitive supplied by the frozen reference semantics.
   254	- Semantic role: models the external standard-library operation and supplies
   255	  its result value; it is not program-defined code.
   256	- Domain: the exact single-positional-argument `hashlib.md5` syntax used by
   257	  `solution.py`, a modeled string argument, and a zero-argument `hexdigest`.
   258	- Matched context: the active `<k>` item with an arbitrary preserved suffix;
   259	  no other cell is matched or changed by these rules.
   260	- Justification scope: conditional trust that this supplied route denotes the
   261	  imported CPython `hashlib.md5` binding for this exact source, which does not
   262	  rebind `hashlib`.
   263	- Context containment: the source has the exact imported name, call arity,
   264	  receiver, method, and continuation shape. The route evaluates `E` first and
   265	  preserves its continuation.
   266	- State footprint: reads/evaluates the argument in `<k>`; writes no scope,
   267	  heap, allocation, stack, return, exception, or exit-code cell.
   268	- Value influence: determines the entire nonempty returned string and its
   269	  postcondition.
   270	- Value justification: the named external contract says
   271	  `md5hexCodes(CS)` denotes the lowercase MD5 hex digest of the encoded input.
   272	  There is deliberately no K equation or evaluator that assumes a particular
   273	  digest.
   274	- Dependents: `SPEC.nonempty-input`; the empty-input claim does not depend on
   275	  the digest value.
   276	- Control validation: Haskell `krun` completes the full empty/nonempty smoke
   277	  program with the continuation and all state cells intact. The target claims
   278	  execute the same fixed route.
   279	- Value validation: `test_solution.py` compares the executable CPython
   280	  implementation with the independent OpenSSL 3.0.2 MD5 engine on 213 cases
   281	  with zero mismatches. This is finite evidence, not a universal K theorem.
   282	
   283	### Supplied string-encoding model
   284	
   285	- Component: fixed rule
   286	  `applyMethod(str(CS), "encode", str(_), .Vals) => str(CS)`.
   287	- Class: trusted primitive/model abstraction supplied by the frozen semantics.
   288	- Effect: its value feeds `md5hexCodes`, but it changes no control or state
   289	  cell.
   290	- Exact conditional scope: faithful to UTF-8 for ASCII code sequences. For
   291	  non-ASCII Python strings it is a recorded model boundary.
   292	- Dependents: `SPEC.nonempty-input`.
   293	- Evidence: `π` has source code point `[960]` but UTF-8 bytes `[207, 128]`.
   294	  The Python implementation itself uses UTF-8 correctly and the differential
   295	  corpus includes Unicode.
   296	
   297	### Supplied import/binding abstraction
   298	
   299	- Component: the fixed semantics treats ordinary `Import` as a no-op and
   300	  recognizes the exact syntax `Name("hashlib").md5` directly.
   301	- Class: trusted external binding/control abstraction.
   302	- Effect: pins the external binding and bypasses a modeled module object; it
   303	  changes no user-visible state represented by the claims.
   304	- Exact conditional scope: this source executes `import hashlib` before the
   305	  function call and never rebinds `hashlib`.
   306	- Dependents: `SPEC.nonempty-input`.
   307	- Evidence: exact source/claim syntax, successful fixed-semantics control
   308	  execution, and the body-sensitivity rejection. No claim is made that this
   309	  import model is adequate for arbitrary Python programs or rebinding.
   310	
   311	## Empirically supported facts
   312	
   313	- CPython `solution.py` agrees with OpenSSL's separate MD5 implementation on
   314	  213 explicit and deterministic-random cases, including empty input, the
   315	  prompt example, standard MD5 vectors, NUL/whitespace, long ASCII, and
   316	  Unicode. Mismatch count: zero.
   317	- The prompt example evaluates to
   318	  `3e25960a79dbc69b674cd4ec67a72c62`.
   319	- LLVM `krun` concretely validates the empty branch and all control/state
   320	  cleanup. Haskell `krun` validates the same control path for nonempty input
   321	  while retaining the opaque digest term.
   322	- These are finite observations. Universal correctness in K is only the
   323	  conditional symbolic theorem stated above.
   324	
   325	## Excluded behavior
   326	
   327	- Non-string Python arguments are outside the prompt's stated string domain.
   328	- The proof does not derive the MD5 compression algorithm, collision
   329	  resistance, cryptographic security, or the concrete value of
   330	  `md5hexCodes`; that value is the supplied named trust boundary.
   331	- The supplied K string model does not reproduce UTF-8 byte expansion for
   332	  non-ASCII code points. The actual Python implementation does; the formal
   333	  K-to-CPython correspondence on those values is conditional on this recorded
   334	  model boundary.
   335	- The supplied LLVM backend cannot evaluate nonempty `md5hexCodes` terms and
   336	  exits `113`; this is expected from its `no-evaluators` declaration. The
   337	  positive symbolic target proofs do not use the LLVM evaluator.
   338	- The K workflow proves partial correctness. No separate asymptotic cost,
   339	  resource bound, or total-correctness theorem is claimed.
